## Organize your manuscript

Use the *Binder* on the left to arrange your writing. A typical novel has a Book, chapters
inside it, and scenes inside each chapter. You can also write directly in chapters.

Each item has a *type*, such as Chapter or Note. This tells Skribisto how to use it when
exporting your book; a plain folder does not become a chapter just because it holds scenes.

Every item that carries prose has a main text and a synopsis side by side: plan in one
pane, draft in the other. Books, parts, note folders and plain folders carry a synopsis
but no main text.

## The types you can create

Right-click an item in the binder and choose *Add*.

- *Book*: a manuscript, with its chapters and scenes.
- *Part*: gathers chapters under one heading.
- *Chapter*: runs until the next chapter, the next part, or the end of the book.
- *Scene*: your writing, with its own synopsis.
- *Note*: text and a synopsis, for research, asides or reminders.
- *Story bible entry…*: a note for a character, a place or an object, created with its
  tags and alternative names already filled in.
- *Note folder*: groups notes together, with a synopsis but no main text.
- *Folder*: a plain container with a synopsis and no prose of its own.

## Two shapes of chapter

- A *Chapter folder* holds scenes underneath it in the binder.
- A *Flat chapter* is a single item you write straight into, with no scenes below.

To choose the shape *new* chapters take, open *Settings ▸ Work: <project> ▸ Structure* and
set *Flat chapters*. On means single items, off means folders.

## Change a scene, chapter or folder

Right-click the item and choose *Convert to* to see the available types. For example,
you can turn a scene into a note, or a chapter folder into a flat chapter.
The title and text are kept when the new type can hold them.

- A folder must be empty before it can become a single item. Move or trash its contents
  first.
- A conversion with nowhere to put existing text is refused, and says what would be lost.

## Where one book ends

A project can hold several books, one after another in the same binder. A book runs from
where it starts until the first of three things: an *End of Book* item, the start of the
next book, or the end of its binder.

## Paratext: in the book, not in the story

A *Paratext* belongs to the book but not to its story: a preface, a dedication, an
afterword, a colophon. It exports where you put it, and never counts towards the word
count.

A *Paratext folder* keeps paratexts clear of the rest of the binder.

New to Skribisto? [Getting started](:help-getting-started) covers opening and saving a
project first.
